// cursor-boundary.test.maxon — hover and `textDocument/definition` resolve ONE cursor position, and this
// is what says so.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A CARET AT A TOKEN BOUNDARY TOUCHES TWO TOKENS, AND THE TWO METHODS ONCE DISAGREED ABOUT WHICH
// ONE IT NAMES.** MEASURED against the server, caret one past `name`: in `let z = name///note` definition
// jumped to `name` while hover answered NOTHING, and in `let z = name#if os(Windows)` hover described the
// `#if`. An editor that jumps somewhere it will not describe is the defect.
//
// ⭐ The cure was one resolver, not two agreeing ones — `LspSymbols.lspTokenAtCursor`. The rule it
// settles: an identifier ENDING at the offset beats a token BEGINNING there. Half-open containment counts
// `offset == start` as "inside", which is the caret at a token's LEFT edge rather than within it, so
// containment-first is what made hover describe the directive the author had just typed past. Strict
// containment and ending-at-an-identifier are mutually exclusive, so this boundary is the ONLY position
// the two rules ever decide differently — which is why nothing else in the suite exposed it.
//
// See `LspClient.maxon` for why every session is released before anything is asserted.

// `name` is followed IMMEDIATELY by a `///` comment, so the offset one past `name` is also the comment
// token's first byte. Only a fixture like this puts two SPANNED tokens on one offset — a punctuator
// carries no span, which is why the ordinary `arr.` case never exposed the disagreement.
function boundaryDocCommentFixture() returns String
	return String.from(b"let name = 1
let z = name///note")
end 'boundaryDocCommentFixture'

// The same boundary against a KEYWORD rather than prose: the case that made hover describe a
// conditional-compilation directive where the author was pointing at a name.
function boundaryDirectiveFixture() returns String
	return String.from(b"let name = 1
let z = name#if os(Windows)
#endif")
end 'boundaryDirectiveFixture'

// One past the `e` of `name` on the second line — `let z = name` is twelve characters.
//          111111111122
// 0123456789012345678901
// let z = name///note
let BoundaryCursorLine = 1 as LspCoordinate
let BoundaryCursorCharacter = 12 as LspCoordinate

// The line `let name = 1` declares it.
let BoundaryDeclarationLine = 0 as LspCoordinate

test 'hover and definition name the same token at a boundary'
	try boundaryBothNameTheDeclaration(boundaryDocCommentFixture(), what: "a `///` comment")
	try boundaryBothNameTheDeclaration(boundaryDirectiveFixture(), what: "a `#if` directive")
end 'hover and definition name the same token at a boundary'

function boundaryBothNameTheDeclaration(source String, what String) throws TestFailure
	try expectDefinitionAt(source, line: BoundaryCursorLine, character: BoundaryCursorCharacter, startLine: BoundaryDeclarationLine, note: "definition at the end of `name`, where {what} begins, must reach the declaration of `name`.")

	let rendering = try hoverRendering(source, line: BoundaryCursorLine, character: BoundaryCursorCharacter)
	let declaration = "let name = 1"

	try Expect.contains(rendering, needle: declaration, message: "hover at the end of `name`, where {what} begins, described something else. Definition jumps to `name` from this very position, so a hover describing anything else is an editor jumping somewhere it will not describe.")
end 'boundaryBothNameTheDeclaration'
